	"description": [
		"Frederic Jameson has described conspiracy theory as a form of cognitive mapping that attempts \"to think a system so vast that it cannot be encompassed by the natural and historically developed categories of perception.\" Engaging with both literature and film, this course invites students to think about how conspiracy narratives reflect and manage the anxieties and fears that accompany the totalizing conditions of late-stage capitalism, state surveillance and violence, media consolidation, and so on. Students study the form these narratives take, while paying special attention to allegory, detection, and conspiracy's \"paranoid style.\" Topics include alien abduction, the assassination plot, government overreach, space exploration, mind control, medical treatment, and ecological disaster. Prerequisite: WRIT 120 or equivalent."
	],
